TAMLINUX-PACKAGES

Public delivery definitions for Tamlinux
<https://github.com/greenermoose/tamlinux>, tamlinux-tools
<https://github.com/greenermoose/tamlinux-tools>, tam
<https://github.com/greenermoose/tam>, and libtam
<https://github.com/greenermoose/libtam>.

This repository records exact package assemblies (bundle recipes, locks and
<deliveries/>) for Test and Run. Current develop version is in <VERSION>. Gate
A closed with released 0.4.8 and Blue Sparkler released as 0.5.12; develop
continues on 0.6 (KBN WB). Product source lives in the component repos; this
repo selects pins and builds them into tam-bundle archives.

The deployment contract <docs/deployment.md> requires Test and Run to install
an exact, recorded bundle at the same locations. Native bundles <docs/bundles.md>
describes the recipes. Login is console-first <docs/console-startup.md>. See
docs/README <docs/README> for the operational doc index.

From a checkout:

    python3 -B -m unittest discover -s tests

Nix and Home Manager were the previous delivery and are being retired. Until
then the flake still builds (nix flake check; nix build .#example-home) for
machines that keep a Nix fallback; homeManagerModules.default and
tamlinux.shell options serve only that path. No new work targets it.

The clean-host contract <docs/clean-host.md> declares native Arch/Hyprland
prerequisites and audits built outputs for missing commands and inherited
identifiers. It is a proof bar for independent delivery, not a claim that
every candidate already passes it.

On the Nix path, the developer baseline installs Bash, editing, C compilation/linking with libc
headers, Make, Git, secure downloads and certificate trust. A consumer can set
tamlinux.developer.nativeProvided = true only after verifying equivalent host
tools. lib.packageSets pkgs provides developer, terminalGames, and optional
userTools lists; personal applications, vendor editors and their overrides
stay with consumers.

Source behavior and ordinary build interfaces belong in their component
repositories. Bundle recipes, native package adapters, dependency selection,
assembly and delivery verification belong here.

GNU GPL v3 or later. See <LICENSE> and AI provenance <AI_PROVENANCE.md>.

Software defect tracker <bugs/README-bugs.md>: report reproducible defects
beside their source.
